## Problem

PHPUnit CI has been failing on `ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandlerTest::test_namespace_decision_overrides_heuristic` since at least the last pulse cycle:

```
Failed asserting that null is not null.
/tests/SdAiAgent/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandlerTest.php:160
```

Root cause: `stubs/wordpress-7-runtime.php` contains empty stub bodies for the WP 7.0 Abilities API. Both `wp_register_ability()` and `wp_get_ability()` always return `null`; the in-memory registry needed for tests does not exist.

Additionally, PHPCS reports 3 auto-fixable violations in the same file detected in CI run 25903499038:

- `includes/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandler.php:126` — `Generic.Formatting.MultipleStatementAlignment.NotSameWarning` (auto-fixable)
- `includes/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandler.php:139` — `PEAR.Functions.FunctionCallSignature.ContentAfterOpenBracket` (auto-fixable)
- `includes/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandler.php:144` — `PEAR.Functions.FunctionCallSignature.CloseBracketLine` (auto-fixable)

Related parent epic: For #1404

## Files to modify

- `stubs/wordpress-7-runtime.php` — implement in-memory ability registry. The `WP_Ability` constructor needs `$this->name = $name` so `get_name()` returns the correct value. The registry functions (`wp_register_ability`, `wp_get_ability`, `wp_unregister_ability`, `wp_get_abilities`) must share a static variable. Since PHP `static` variables are per-function, use a private registry class or a named global array to share state across all four functions.

  Example pattern (adapt to codebase style):

  ```php
  // Within the `if ( ! class_exists( 'WP_Ability' ) ) :` guard block
  $_wp_ability_registry = [];

  function wp_register_ability( string $name, array $args ): ?WP_Ability {
      global $_wp_ability_registry;
      $ability = new WP_Ability( $name, $args );
      $_wp_ability_registry[ $name ] = $ability;
      return $ability;
  }

  function wp_get_ability( string $name ): ?WP_Ability {
      global $_wp_ability_registry;
      return $_wp_ability_registry[ $name ] ?? null;
  }

  function wp_unregister_ability( string $name ): ?WP_Ability {
      global $_wp_ability_registry;
      $ability = $_wp_ability_registry[ $name ] ?? null;
      unset( $_wp_ability_registry[ $name ] );
      return $ability;
  }

  function wp_get_abilities(): array {
      global $_wp_ability_registry;
      return array_values( $_wp_ability_registry );
  }
  ```

  Also add `$this->name = $name;` to `WP_Ability::__construct()`.

- `includes/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandler.php` — run `vendor/bin/phpcbf includes/Admin/ThirdPartyAbilityNoticeHandler.php` to auto-fix the 3 marked PHPCS violations.

## Reference pattern

Check how other WP 7.0 stub functions are guarded in `stubs/wordpress-7-runtime.php` (the `if ( ! function_exists(...) ):` blocks). Replicate the same guard pattern for the registry variable.

## Verification

```bash
